  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times Z\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in Z}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/8e677c814756c4818c25da450d789c974787df60cf4bb18017a96593d3ecc180.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/f99beeb00c070048ee4ee7c1b6c9e077d7d4e0f3fd9e63aa12b5b58588e14dbb.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $\u2026{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},\u2026$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times Z & =\u2026{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup \u2026 \\\\  & ={\u22c3}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times Z$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png)",
